Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer asks for a refund because they changed their mind, approve it if the request is made up to 14 days after delivery. A request 10 days after delivery is allowed, and so is one 2 days after delivery.
  2. Whenever a customer reports that a delivered item arrived damaged within 90 days of delivery, accept the report without requiring a photo and refund the full item price plus the shipping fee. For example, a cracked-screen phone reported 40 days after delivery is accepted with item and shipping fully refunded. Rahul's ₹1,200 item plus ₹100 delivery refunds ₹1,300 when the goods are damaged.
  3. Whenever an order is refunded because the customer changed their mind, refund only the item price and never the delivery fee. Rahul's ₹1,200 item plus ₹100 delivery order refunds ₹1,200 when no discount code is involved. The delivery fee is refunded in addition to the item price only when the goods arrive damaged.
  4. Whenever an order was paid with a discount code and is refunded, refund the amount the customer actually paid, not the undiscounted price. For example, a ₹5,000 order paid at ₹4,000 after a 20% coupon refunds ₹4,000.
  5. Whenever a customer returns only some of the items in an order, refund the amount paid for the returned items (item price times quantity, minus any discount code). Make no deduction and charge no restocking fee. For example, returning one ₹1,200 item from a ₹3,600 order of 3 items refunds ₹1,200. The usual rules on days, shipping and payment method still apply.
  6. Whenever a refund is paid, return it to the original payment method with no handling charge. Card orders are refunded to the card and store-credit orders as store credit. For example, Meena's ₹1,500 card order, changed her mind 2 days after delivery, is refunded to her card in full.
  7. Whenever a refund is requested for an order that has already been refunded, refuse it, because an order can never be refunded twice. Whenever a refund is requested for an order that has not yet been delivered, no refund rule is defined, so the behavior is unspecified.
  8. Whenever a refund request has no reason, or only the reason 'not happy', do not approve, deny or pay it automatically. Put it on hold, issue no refund and move no money, and ask the owner to decide. Money moves only after the owner has decided.
  9. Whenever a refund amount, loyalty points, or the average on the daily report is calculated, round down (truncate) to whole rupees, never to the nearest rupee. For example, a refund of ₹1,249.75 pays back ₹1,249. Whenever a refund comes to less than 50 rupees, pay nothing (the refund amount is 0), except for damaged goods.
  10. Whenever an order is paid by card, award one loyalty point for every full ₹100 paid for the items after the discount code is subtracted, ignoring any leftover. For example, ₹1,250 paid for the items earns 12 points. Shipping, delivery and tax earn nothing. Orders under ₹500 earn no points. Orders paid fully by store credit earn no points. Final-sale orders earn double points. Whenever an order is partly paid with store credit, the owner has not set a rule for how it counts, so hold it for his answer before implementing it. Do not use ₹1,450 for Ramesh's order.
  11. Whenever an order that earned points is partly refunded, do not change the customer's points on your own. Hold the points as they are and ask the owner to decide what happens to them. For example, Deepa's ₹1,000 refund on a ₹4,000 order that earned 40 points leaves her 40 points until the owner decides.
  12. Whenever the daily figures are calculated, apply no rule to cancelled or fully refunded orders until the owner decides. 'Orders' counts every order in the list, and 'Revenue' is the card orders' totals (items after discount, plus shipping). Whenever the day includes a cancelled or fully refunded order, hold the report back and ask the owner to decide how those orders appear. Do not guess a treatment.
  13. Whenever a day has no orders at all, the daily sales summary shows an order count of 0, revenue of 0 and an average of 0. It carries no value over from a previous day and shows no message or notice.
